WEBVTT

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:04.220
This pull request adds a new
indicator to a real trading repo.

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:10.530
An AI team planned it, wrote it, tested it
and reviewed it. You designed that team.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:12.950
This is Tvashtr.

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:18.120
Now build the team. Start from a template.

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:24.760
Every agent is a blank box: its job is
whatever you write in its instructions.

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:28.770
Then wire in the checks.
You approve the spec.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:33.410
Tvashtr runs your tests, and a
failure loops back to the engineer.

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:36.280
The reviewer can send work back too.

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:40.790
Guardrails scan for secrets and
block edits to the servers folder.

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:43.610
Now give the team a task.

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:48.500
Pick the repo, the branch, and the
one folder it may touch. Launch.

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:54.240
First, the product manager writes
a spec, and the run waits for you.

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:56.810
The spec is a real document.

00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:01.450
Fix a line, approve, and only
then is any code written.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:06.940
The engineer works on its own copy of
your repo, on your Claude subscription.

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:09.900
Then Tvashtr runs your tests itself.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:14.990
The first build fails, and the last twelve
lines go straight back to the engineer.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:19.720
Round two passes: six
hundred and sixty-five tests.

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:24.050
Only then does the reviewer, on Grok,
check the change against the spec.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:29.230
It approves, citing Tvashtr's test
result, not the engineer's word.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:36.520
A secret scan and a forbidden-paths check
both pass. Tvashtr opens the pull request.

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:39.920
Done in ten minutes,
on your subscriptions.

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:44.090
Change an agent, and the
team keeps both versions.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:48.120
Then compare them on a real task,
taking turns on your subscription.

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:52.440
Version two did better: its
reviewer passed one more test.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.570
On your desktop, or on the web.

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:59.560
Tvashtr. Your team. Your rules.

